{"subjectId":"java","id":"completablefuture","title":"CompletableFuture","group":"Concurrency","order":62,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>CompletableFuture</code> composes asynchronous steps into a pipeline — transform, chain, combine and recover — without blocking a thread on each <code>get()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;</strong> — a <code>Future</code> you can complete explicitly <em>and</em> a <code>CompletionStage</code>: attach callbacks (<code>thenApply</code>, <code>thenCompose</code>, …) that run when the value is ready. Async methods run on <code>ForkJoinPool.commonPool()</code> unless you pass an <code>Executor</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-join\"></i>Transform and combine</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; price = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; 200);\n        CompletableFuture&lt;Double&gt; discount = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; 0.9);\n\n        double total = price\n            .thenCombine(discount, (p, d) -&gt; p * d)   // wait for BOTH, merge\n            .thenApply(x -&gt; x + 15)                   // transform the value (shipping)\n            .join();                                  // block for the final result\n        System.out.println(\"total = \" + total);\n\n        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; \"order-42\")\n            .thenApply(String::toUpperCase)\n            .thenAccept(id -&gt; System.out.println(\"placed \" + id))   // consume, returns CF&lt;Void&gt;\n            .thenRun(() -&gt; System.out.println(\"email queued\"))      // no input, no output\n            .join();\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>total = 195.0\nplaced ORDER-42\nemail queued</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-branch\"></i>thenApply vs thenCompose</h3>\n<p><code>thenApply</code> is <code>map</code>: the function returns a plain value. <code>thenCompose</code> is <code>flatMap</code>: the function itself returns a <code>CompletableFuture</code>, and you want the inner result — not a nested <code>CompletableFuture&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.List;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static CompletableFuture&lt;String&gt; findUser(int id) {\n        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; \"user\" + id);\n    }\n\n    static CompletableFuture&lt;List&lt;String&gt;&gt; findOrders(String user) {\n        return CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; List.of(user + \":book\", user + \":pen\"));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;String&gt; orders = findUser(7)\n            .thenCompose(Main::findOrders)        // async step depending on the previous one\n            .join();\n        System.out.println(orders);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[user7:book, user7:pen]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Function shape</th><th>Analogy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>thenApply(f)</code></td><td><code>T -&gt; U</code></td><td><code>map</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>thenCompose(f)</code></td><td><code>T -&gt; CompletionStage&lt;U&gt;</code></td><td><code>flatMap</code> — sequential async calls</td></tr><tr><td><code>thenCombine(other, f)</code></td><td><code>(T, U) -&gt; V</code></td><td>zip two independent futures</td></tr><tr><td><code>thenAccept(c)</code> / <code>thenRun(r)</code></td><td><code>T -&gt; void</code> / <code>() -&gt; void</code></td><td>side effect at the end</td></tr><tr><td><code>allOf(cfs...)</code></td><td>→ <code>CompletableFuture&lt;Void&gt;</code></td><td>wait for all</td></tr><tr><td><code>anyOf(cfs...)</code></td><td>→ <code>CompletableFuture&lt;Object&gt;</code></td><td>first to finish wins</td></tr><tr><td><code>...Async</code> variants</td><td>same, but callback runs on the pool</td><td>don't hog the completing thread</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>Waiting for many: allOf</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.List;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List&lt;CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt;&gt; calls = List.of(\"alpha\", \"beta\", \"gamma\").stream()\n            .map(s -&gt; CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; s.length()))\n            .toList();\n\n        CompletableFuture.allOf(calls.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; lengths = calls.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList();  // all done: no waiting\n        System.out.println(lengths + \" sum=\" + lengths.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[5, 4, 5] sum=14</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Handling errors</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\nimport java.util.concurrent.CompletionException;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        CompletableFuture&lt;Integer&gt; failing = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; Integer.parseInt(\"x\"));\n\n        int recovered = failing.exceptionally(ex -&gt; -1).join();          // fallback value\n        System.out.println(\"exceptionally -&gt; \" + recovered);\n\n        String handled = failing.handle((value, ex) -&gt;                   // sees success OR failure\n            ex == null ? \"ok \" + value : \"failed: \" + ex.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName()).join();\n        System.out.println(\"handle -&gt; \" + handled);\n\n        try {\n            failing.join();                                              // unchecked wrapper\n        } catch (CompletionException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"join threw CompletionException, cause \" + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName());\n        }\n        try {\n            failing.get();                                               // checked wrapper\n        } catch (ExecutionException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"get threw ExecutionException, cause \" + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName());\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>exceptionally -&gt; -1\nhandle -&gt; failed: NumberFormatException\njoin threw CompletionException, cause NumberFormatException\nget threw ExecutionException, cause NumberFormatException</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Called on</th><th>Can change result?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>exceptionally(fn)</code></td><td>failure only</td><td>Yes — supply a fallback</td></tr><tr><td><code>handle((v, ex) -&gt; ...)</code></td><td>success and failure</td><td>Yes — map either to a new value</td></tr><tr><td><code>whenComplete((v, ex) -&gt; ...)</code></td><td>success and failure</td><td>No — side effects (logging); original result passes through</td></tr><tr><td><code>orTimeout(t, unit)</code> / <code>completeOnTimeout(v, t, unit)</code></td><td>Java 9+</td><td>Fail / default after a timeout</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-server\"></i>Use your own executor</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;\nimport java.util.concurrent.Executors;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try (ExecutorService io = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2, r -&gt; new Thread(r, \"io-worker\"))) {\n            String where = CompletableFuture\n                .supplyAsync(() -&gt; Thread.currentThread().getName(), io)   // runs on our pool\n                .join();\n            System.out.println(\"ran on \" + where);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>ran on io-worker</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The common <code>ForkJoinPool</code> is sized for CPU work (cores − 1 threads) and shared by the whole JVM, including parallel streams. Blocking I/O in <code>supplyAsync(...)</code> without an executor can starve everything else — pass a dedicated executor (or virtual threads).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>join() vs get()?</summary><div><p>Both block for the result. <code>get()</code> throws checked <code>InterruptedException</code> and <code>ExecutionException</code>; <code>join()</code> throws unchecked <code>CompletionException</code>, which makes it usable inside lambdas and stream pipelines.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>thenApply vs thenCompose?</summary><div><p><code>thenApply</code> maps the result with a function returning a value. <code>thenCompose</code> is for a function that returns another <code>CompletionStage</code> — it flattens, so dependent async calls chain as <code>CF&lt;U&gt;</code> instead of <code>CF&lt;CF&lt;U&gt;&gt;</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Future vs CompletableFuture?</summary><div><p>A plain <code>Future</code> only lets you poll or block with <code>get()</code>. <code>CompletableFuture</code> can be completed manually (<code>complete</code>, <code>completeExceptionally</code>), supports non-blocking callbacks, composition (<code>thenCompose</code>, <code>thenCombine</code>, <code>allOf</code>) and error recovery (<code>exceptionally</code>, <code>handle</code>).</p></div></details></section>"}